
/*
PROBLEM: KOKO EATING BANANAS
============================

APPROACH: BINARY SEARCH ON THE ANSWER

PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
We are given an array 'arr', where each element represents the number
of bananas in a pile, and an integer 'max_hours', which is the maximum
time available to eat all the bananas.

We need to find the minimum eating speed, in bananas per hour, that
allows us to finish all the piles within max_hours.

Koko eats at a fixed speed. If a pile contains fewer bananas than
the eating speed, she still needs one full hour to finish that pile.
She cannot split an hour between two different piles.

CALCULATING THE HOURS:
For each pile, we calculate the hours needed using ceiling division:

    hours = ceil(arr[i] / speed)

We can calculate this without floating-point arithmetic by dividing
the pile size by speed and adding 1 if there is a remainder.

For example, if a pile has 10 bananas and speed is 3, the hours
needed are 4 because 10 / 3 = 3 with a remainder of 1.

If a pile has 9 bananas and speed is 3, the hours needed are 3
because the division is exact.

HELPER FUNCTION f():
The helper function checks whether a given eating speed can finish
all the piles within max_hours. It calculates the hours needed for
each pile and adds them to total_hours.

If total_hours exceeds max_hours at any point, we return false
immediately. There is no need to check the remaining piles because
the total hours cannot decrease.

If all piles are processed and the total hours are within the limit,
the function returns true, meaning the speed is sufficient.

WHY BINARY SEARCH WORKS:
A higher eating speed requires fewer or equal hours to finish the
piles. A lower speed requires more or equal hours.

Therefore, if a speed is sufficient, every higher speed is also
sufficient. If a speed is too slow, every lower speed is also too
slow.

This pattern allows us to use binary search to find the minimum
speed that works.

SEARCH RANGE:
The minimum speed is 1 because Koko must eat at least one banana
per hour.

We set the upper limit to the sum of all bananas. At this speed,
Koko can finish each pile in one hour, so the total time needed is
the number of piles.

This upper limit is guaranteed to work when max_hours is at least
the number of piles, as required by the problem.

BINARY SEARCH:
We calculate mid as the eating speed to test and call f() to check
whether it is sufficient.

If mid is sufficient, we save it in ans and search the left half
for a smaller speed by setting end = mid - 1.

If mid is insufficient, we need a higher speed, so we set
start = mid + 1.

We repeat until start > end. The smallest sufficient speed is
stored in ans and returned.

COMPLEXITY:
Let n be the number of piles and S be the sum of all bananas.

Time Complexity: O(n * log S), because each check takes O(n) time
and binary search takes O(log S) iterations.

Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1), because we use only a fixed
number of extra variables.
*/


// ORIGINAL CODE WITH SHORT COMMENTS

class Solution {
public:
    // Check whether the given speed finishes all piles in time.
    bool f(vector<int>& arr, int max_hours, int speed) {
        int n = arr.size();
        long long total_hours = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            long long ele = arr[i];

            total_hours += (ele / speed);

            // Add one hour if the division has a remainder.
            if (ele % speed) {
                total_hours++;
            }

            // Stop if the allowed time is exceeded.
            if (total_hours > max_hours) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return total_hours <= max_hours;
    }

    // Find the minimum eating speed using binary search.
    int minEatingSpeed(vector<int>& arr, int max_hours) {
        int n = arr.size();

        long long start = 1;
        long long end = accumulate(arr.begin(), arr.end(), 0LL);
        long long ans = 1e18;

        while (start <= end) {
            long long mid = start + (end - start) / 2;

            bool flag = f(arr, max_hours, mid);

            if (flag) {
                ans = mid;       // Valid speed: try a smaller one.
                end = mid - 1;
            } else {
                start = mid + 1; // Too slow: increase the speed.
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
